WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.810
all right, let's get into audience questions.
For the week. Very timely. One from Joe. Joe

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:10.000
says, Hey David, I'm glad you're recovering
from surgery. Can you talk about your recent

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.269
healthcare experience related

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:18.200
Bernie Sanders and then compare and contrast
both with Americans who are not insured or

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.539
under insured? Yes, so listen, I mean there's
a lot here. If you don't, don't know. I recently

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:29.660
had an emergency appendectomy. I missed last
week's Thursday show and some of the Friday

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.510
show. Some of the elements of it were, were
taped earlier in the week and uh, I have insurance

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:40.219
and I was in a position where I went into
an emergency room. I actually, I went to urgent

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:45.679
care first and was told I was fine and then
hours later was in an emergency room anyway

00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:52.300
and had immediate, you know, cat scan, blood
tests, all of that stuff was scheduled for

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:59.389
surgery. Once it became clear I had appendicitis.
And I'm guessing that part of the reason why

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:05.760
it all just happened was that the hospital,
because it's a network hospital with the network

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.170
I'm associated with already had my insurance
information, knew I had insurance and there

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.960
were no questions about it.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:20.350
Now, that being said, I have a silver level
plan here in Massachusetts. It costs me $421

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:27.100
per month and it still has a $2,000 annual
deductible. I believe it's $1,000 deductible

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:33.500
for imaging. So that cat scan, I'm already
been, I've been billed $302 for that cat scan.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.640
I'm anticipating that the remaining 1700 or
so of my deductible will be billed to me.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.520
I don't know in the end what the procedure
will cost. I've read that appendectomies in

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.460
the U S can range between 1500 to $180,000
depending on, I don't even know what, which

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:57.430
is crazy in and of itself, but I, I know,
I mean, listen, I have a savings account.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:03.800
If I'm prepared to have to pay the $2,000
and I know that I might have to, uh, I think

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:10.810
my annual out of pocket maximum is 7,500.
I have that in savings, so I know it's not

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.609
going to bankrupt me.

00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:18.599
Now, if I were not insured or underinsured,
the circumstances would be different. And

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:22.910
also they would be different if I didn't have
money in savings to self insure against the

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:29.239
maximum annual costs of the plan. Now in the
hospital I had hours during which I was just

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:33.829
looking around, just sitting there and there
are signs that say whether or not you have

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.329
insurance, and this is, I think this is Massachusetts
law, but I think most States have something

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:46.260
similar regardless of insurance status or
ability to pay. Uh, I have to be given a stabilizing

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:54.099
treatment and if necessary, a transfer to
another facility. Now if you go in with acute

00:02:54.099 --> 00:03:00.701
appendicitis and you don't have insurance,
what counts as stabilizing treatment? The

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:06.099
appendix has to come out or you can try to
treat it in some cases with antibiotics. Although

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.650
in my case they said, you know, you really
should have it removed.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:15.560
Is anything short of an appendectomy considered
stabilizing for someone with acute appendicitis?

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.779
I don't know the answer to that. Those are
the types of problems that would ultimately

00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:26.730
present themselves for someone who is uninsured
and underinsured by under-insured, I'm guessing

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:32.840
we're talking about a catastrophic plan, which
instead of running for 20 a month, like I

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:38.020
pay, I think it's about half of that. Like
it's still $200 a month. I think, um, what

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:43.599
happens or if you have a subsidized catastrophic
plan where you might, because of your income

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:49.629
only pay $60 a month. But if you go in there,
you might have a $7,000 deductible that has

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:56.110
to be met. What happens? You're screwed basically.
Um, do you go into bankruptcy? I don't know.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:02.739
Do you go into this debt collection, industrial
complex and then potentially start paying

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:07.559
interest on top of the bills? I mean, this
is why the system doesn't make sense because

00:04:07.559 --> 00:04:14.709
as soon as you're in that sort of hamster
treadmill as it is effectively, uh, you, are

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:17.730
you going to be even more hampered in your
ability to get out of it?

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.630
Because at that point you have trouble potentially
making your car payment. If your car gets

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:27.320
repossessed. How do you get to work? You need
to go to work in order to make the money to

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:32.800
pay off the medical debt. And you very quickly
start seeing the snowball effect and the negative

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.040
externalities of this. So, I mean, listen,
my situation, Bernie's Bernie, I'm sure has

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.060
far better health insurance than I have. It's
not good. It's not good when you consider

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:49.150
that, when you don't have that, um, and you
don't have some money saved, which I'm fortunate

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:55.330
enough to have. You are, would I have delayed
even going to urgent care or the emergency

00:04:55.330 --> 00:05:01.020
room? I had a $55 copay at urgent care would
ended up, we ended up just sending me home

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.470
with appendicitis instead of saying we should
actually do some imaging here. You probably

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:11.980
delay going, which can lead to a burst or
perforated appendix and then it gets much

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:13.670
more expensive.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:16.310
It's a mess. It's extraordinarily depressing
and, uh, it's just, it's gotta be fixed. This

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:17.310
next question is, I mean, I'm taking it as
a real question, but it is also, I can tell

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:17.325
someone who just doesn't understand what the
hell is going on in the United States.

